Exactly. The map should become a queryable spatial canvas, not just a place-search box.

Your “show me 70 miles” example actually reveals two different geometries:

\text{70-mile radius} \neq \text{70 miles of driving}

A radius gives you a literal circle around your location. “About 70 miles by car” should generate an isochrone / road-network contour—an irregular boundary showing everywhere reachable within that distance, potentially adjusted for traffic, road class, tolls, ferries, etc.

Then the AI layer should let you say things like:

* “Show me everything about an hour away without using the interstate.”
* “Draw the farthest I could get on half a tank.”
* “Find a beach inside this area that has bathrooms, food nearby, and isn’t crowded.”
* “Show me the overlap between 45 minutes from me and 45 minutes from my brother.”
* “Highlight places that feel halfway between city and wilderness.”
* “Show me where I could drive until sunset and still make it home before 10.”
* “Circle every place I’ve been in the last year and show me the largest unexplored gap.”
* “Give me three weird side quests along this route that add less than 15 minutes.”
* “Find somewhere roughly 70 miles away, but make the drive itself interesting.”
* “Show me this same map as walking distance / biking effort / boat travel / straight-line distance.”

And crucially, descriptive search should work on what is currently visible:

“That cluster near the river—what is it?”
“Remove all the chain restaurants.”
“Why are there fewer roads over here?”
“Show me the quietest route through this section.”
“Make this whole patch my search boundary.”
“What am I not noticing?”

So instead of:

\text{text query} \rightarrow \text{list of destinations}

you get:

\text{language}
\leftrightarrow
\text{map geometry}
\leftrightarrow
\text{constraints}
\leftrightarrow
\text{live world}

The strongest version would also understand gestures: circle an area with your finger and say, “Find somewhere in here that fits us.” Draw a line and say, “Make a scenic route roughly along this.” Pin two locations and say, “Find the interesting middle.”

That’s really the missing interface: AI as a spatial copilot, where the map itself becomes editable through language, imagination, and intent—not merely an index of addresses.